00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:33.974
Within the little droplets of water we study
each week on Journey to the Microcosmos,

00:00:33.974 --> 00:00:40.860
we have seen all the chaotic aspects of life that
we experience on a macro scale unfold between

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.379
the tiniest of organisms.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:48.870
And through them, we’ve learned plenty of
lessons as stories of resilience and adaptation

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.334
unfold before us, like little microscopic
Aesop’s fables.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.592
But Aesop probably would not have come up
with one of our favorite morals of the microcosmos:

00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:06.389
never let anyone convince you that butts,
immortality, or sex

00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:09.000
are easy to define or describe.

00:01:11.191 --> 00:01:16.540
Now if we were to write a fable to get this moral
across, it would have to star the freshwater

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:22.210
cnidarian called the hydra, one of our favorite
stars of the microcosmos.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:28.420
Because in the hydra, the question of butts
connects to the ambiguities of immortality,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.420
which in turn relates to the befuddling matter
of sexual reproduction.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:41.617
And in some species, it builds to a fantastic
reality where sex spells the beginning of the end.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.591
But that’s jumping ahead.

00:01:44.591 --> 00:01:47.808
Let’s start with what's usually the end
of the tale:

00:01:47.808 --> 00:01:52.071
the butt, which in the hydra also double as
the mouth.

00:01:52.071 --> 00:01:57.610
And for hydras, defining the mouth and the
butt is a bit of a complicated affair because much

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.860
of the time, there is no opening at all.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:07.640
The area where the mouth belongs is a continuous
sheet of cells that ruptures open and expands

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.378
when the time comes to eat or to poop.

00:02:10.378 --> 00:02:15.241
Imagining for a moment our own bodies 
working this way is, horrifying.

00:02:15.241 --> 00:02:19.959
There’s the fact that food would enter and
exit our bodies from the same location, and

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:24.000
then the wound inflicted from within to make
that possible.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.970
But for hydras, this is casual, a part of
their daily habit of recycling cells and making

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.418
new ones that makes their bodies capable of
handling all sorts of things.

00:02:33.418 --> 00:02:39.630
Their ability to do this is rooted in populations
of stem cells that are able to endlessly divide

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.674
to make different types of cells that can
patch the hydra right up.

00:02:43.674 --> 00:02:49.116
Theoretically, a hydra could replace all of
the cells along its surface within a week.

00:02:49.116 --> 00:02:52.556
Now there’s a number of philosophical 
questions you could ask about this.

00:02:52.556 --> 00:02:58.288
Is the hydra like the hypothetical ship whose
every part has been replaced by a new piece?

00:02:58.288 --> 00:03:03.924
And if so, is a hydra with new cells still
even the same hydra?

00:03:03.924 --> 00:03:09.210
It seems like we would have to say yes, especially
because it maintains the same sequence of DNA

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.650
—that molecular source of identity that
so often seems to define us.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:20.290
But then what to make of a hydra that reproduces
asexually, budding off new little clones of

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.000
itself that swim away carrying that same DNA?

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.812
Are those clones part of the same original
hydra, or are they their own entities?

00:03:29.812 --> 00:03:33.745
We could argue those questions all day, and
perhaps some of you will.

00:03:33.745 --> 00:03:39.879
But we’re asking them not to get to an obvious
answer, but to instead have a little fun with

00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:46.134
the fact that our definitions inevitably fall
short of what nature actually reveals.

00:03:46.134 --> 00:03:51.461
As far as murkiness in nature goes, hydra
butts might be a little unusual.

00:03:51.461 --> 00:03:56.555
But they are practically straightforward
compared to the question of immortality.

00:03:56.555 --> 00:04:01.119
We’ve actually done an episode about immortality
before, and in it we said the following:

00:04:02.862 --> 00:04:08.336
These stem cells may make the hydra biologically
immortal.

00:04:08.336 --> 00:04:13.760
Because its cells are always being replaced,
the hydra is able to avoid the accumulation

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.889
of damage that other multicellular organisms
experience.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:21.000
The hydra doesn’t age.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.469
And so until another force or predator intervenes,
it doesn’t die.

00:04:28.884 --> 00:04:33.063
Well, we sure did say something that sounded
uncomplicated there.

00:04:33.063 --> 00:04:36.486
But, it turns out, we were a little bit wrong.

00:04:36.486 --> 00:04:40.136
Not entirely wrong, just not entirely right
either.

00:04:40.136 --> 00:04:44.660
And this is where the complicated question
of sex comes in.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:50.240
This is Hydra viridissima, a species found
throughout the world and that’s notable

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.460
for the green color it gets thanks to the
Chlorella algae living symbiotically within it.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:57.275
And, along this hydra’s body,

00:04:57.275 --> 00:05:02.356
you can see tiny round structures with 
something wiggling inside.

00:05:02.356 --> 00:05:09.155
Those wiggling bits are immature sperm cells,
waiting to burst from the organ that encases them.

00:05:09.155 --> 00:05:15.352
Eventually the sperm are released by the hydra
into the water in search of eggs to be fertilized.

00:05:15.352 --> 00:05:18.120
Most of the year, none of that is happening.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.420
The Hydra viridissima will reproduce asexually,
budding off its little clones.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:26.787
But when the weather gets warmer, the population
shifts.

00:05:26.787 --> 00:05:32.027
Individuals develop gonads that mark it as
male, female, or hermpaphroditic.

00:05:32.027 --> 00:05:36.493
And for the female hydras, asexual reproduction
is put on pause.

00:05:36.493 --> 00:05:39.760
Now that all sounds very simple and 
like it follows a clear cycle.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.591
But it’s also very specific to this species.

00:05:43.591 --> 00:05:49.620
If you ever want to feel your brain turn completely to
mush, read a few scientific papers about hydra sex.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.433
It’s not that hydra sex itself is particularly
complicated in its mechanics.

00:05:53.433 --> 00:05:57.440
It’s just that it is very difficult to say
any kind of generality about the strategy

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.588
and effect of sexual reproduction in hydras
across species.

00:06:01.588 --> 00:06:05.663
Some species spend most of their life reproducing
asexually.

00:06:05.663 --> 00:06:09.000
Some alternate between asexual and sexual
reproduction.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.090
Some rely on populations of hermaphroditic
individuals, others have a mix, and still

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:17.568
others have individuals that change their sex.

00:06:17.568 --> 00:06:19.906
And that’s just a few of the details.

00:06:19.906 --> 00:06:25.980
Hydra viridissima, for example, are called
“warm crisis” hydras because they tend

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.349
to shift towards sexual reproduction when
the waters get warmer.

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:36.650
In general, a shift from asexual to sexual
reproduction marks an attempt to survive through

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:42.050
variation, creating eggs or offspring that
might be able to weather the challenges of

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.869
an environment that a population of clones
might not.

00:06:45.869 --> 00:06:52.169
And so, for the hydra viridissima, as waters
get warmer, the possibility of drought or

00:06:52.169 --> 00:06:57.931
shifts in their food might signal the need
to become sexual reproducers.

00:06:57.931 --> 00:07:02.120
But for other species of hydra, it’s not warmth
that triggers the switch.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.170
It’s cold, the oncoming winter that makes
their environment harder and informs their

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.031
decision to change to sexual reproduction.

00:07:10.031 --> 00:07:14.960
The species Hydra oligactis, for example,
has become a useful model for researchers

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.750
to study because all the hydras need to be
convinced to reproduce sexually is a little

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.488
chill in the water.

00:07:21.488 --> 00:07:27.830
And yet, hydras are nothing if not wildly
inconsistent with one another, which means

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.599
there is another species, Hydra vulgaris,
that can begin reproducing sexually within

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:37.737
a few days of its birth with little care for 
temperature of the water at all.

00:07:37.737 --> 00:07:43.340
However, the most meaningful inconsistency
of all ties into one of the long-standing

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.629
debates about hydras:

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:47.670
are they immortal?

00:07:51.976 --> 00:07:54.240
So what does immortality have to do with sex?

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.360
Well, for some hydras, nothing.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.791
But for others, it’s everything, including
the end.

00:08:00.791 --> 00:08:03.990
As we said before, hydras can of course die.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.740
There are predators and forces of nature they
cannot escape.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.270
But aging, well, that seems like something
they’ve never had to deal with before because

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:16.547
of that pool of constantly renewing stem cells
within them.

00:08:16.547 --> 00:08:20.780
And for some of the species we’ve talked
about today, like Hydra viridissima and Hydra

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:27.000
vulgaris, those pools of cells just keep on
churning, keeping the animals ageless.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.070
But for Hydra oligactus, our cold-weather
reproducer, a switch to sexual reproduction

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.524
puts a halt to all of that.

00:08:35.524 --> 00:08:41.270
When the temperature drops and the hydra begins
to produce its sperm or eggs, the rest of

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.588
the animal begins to decline as well.

00:08:44.588 --> 00:08:50.330
The cells within it begin to descend into
disarray, and over the time the hydra will

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:53.010
start to eat less and less.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.690
In the lab, they will usually die within months.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.150
But why?

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:04.760
There are a myriad of pathways within the
animal that seem to describe Hydra oligactis’

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:10.251
sudden but slow shift from an immortal to
aging animal.

00:09:10.251 --> 00:09:15.310
And yet these changes in gene expression and
behavior don’t fully explain why this species

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:19.740
is so affected by the switch to sexual reproduction
while others—

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.399
like hydra viridissima—

00:09:21.399 --> 00:09:27.619
are able to switch to and from different methods
of reproduction without any aging at all.

00:09:27.619 --> 00:09:34.010
Buried in that mystery are the answers to
so many of our questions about how aging affects

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.387
our own bodies.

00:09:35.387 --> 00:09:42.060
And perhaps some day we will find those answers, 
nestled in between contradictions created by our own

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:48.300
attempts to define the world, to understand
it as best as we can with the tools we have

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.092
available to us, imperfect as they might be.

00:09:55.158 --> 00:10:01.364
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

